a court in myanmar has convicted ousted  leader ang sanshu chi on three criminal  charges she's been sentenced to four  years in jail tsuchi was on trial for  illegally possessing walkie-talkies and  for breaking coronavirus rules tony  cheng has more from bangkok  we've just heard from her lawyers that  she was found guilty  on all three charges she was facing one  was for import ex for violating import  export regulations the second was for  violating communications laws and the  third was for breaking covert  regulations against myanmar's natural  disasters management law for the import  export infringement she's got two years  for the communications law she's been  sentenced to one year and for the  breaking covert regulations she's got  another two years the first two charges  will be served concurrently so that's  effectively four years in total  we may see as we saw the last time she  was sentenced to four years  a couple of months ago that the military  intervenes and reduces her sentence  which they did last time to two years in  total to be served under house arrest in  nepal the capital of myanmar  but at this stage  this is just adding more years on an  already existing sentence  that will mean she will be at least 80  by the time she's released from  detention  and i think the the message here is very  clear from the military that they are  taking her out of the picture she has  won elections since she's been released  from house arrest  by an absolute landslide in 2019 which  is what prompted the military coup of  february last year and it seems the  military at this stage are very keen to  keep her out of the picture
 

